This product includes software developed by third parties, and renders data
published by third parties.  The two are listed separately and deliberately:
a software licence obliges this PROGRAM, and a data licence obliges its
OUTPUT.  Collapsing them is how the obligation attached to a rendered image
gets forgotten by whoever ships the image.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Software
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

go.mod requires two modules, both from the Go project, and both are written
out in full here so that a third arriving is a visible edit to this file
rather than a quiet line in go.sum.  The dependency target in the Makefile
admits these two BY NAME for the same reason.

golang.org/x/image         Copyright (c) 2009 The Go Authors.  BSD 3-Clause.

golang.org/x/text          Copyright (c) 2009 The Go Authors.  BSD 3-Clause.

  Not imported directly.  It arrives under x/image/font/opentype, which needs
  x/image/font/sfnt, which needs x/text/encoding/charmap to read a font's
  character map.  The command parses a font because it draws place names, and
  the bitmap face it used before had no glyph for most of them: a map of
  Denmark came out reading "S0nder Lindskov".  A map that misspells the places
  on it is worse than a map with none, because the reader cannot tell which
  names are wrong.

  The LIBRARY still parses no font and draws no text without one being handed
  in, so a consumer that supplies its own face links neither the parser nor
  the font data.  This dependency is the command's.
                           The `vector` package rasterizes every filled path
                           this library draws.  It is the only non-standard-
                           library import in the module, and keeping it that
                           way is a deliberate constraint -- see
                           docs/architecture.md, "Zero third-party modules".

There are no other software dependencies.  The PMTiles reader, the Mapbox
Vector Tile decoder, the OpenStreetMap PBF reader and the rasterizer's path
and stroke construction are all written here, for reasons recorded in
docs/architecture.md.


